Campaign workspace-clock, over Private/UI/SFlowVizWorkspace.cpp with filter
FlowViz.UI.Workspace.ClockSeam. Run 2026-08-06 in wind-worktrees/dispatchobs
against 246be5b, 6 arms.

    verdict   arm
    --------  ----------------------------------------------------------
    killed    the clock is never registered -- Play sets a flag nothing
              acts on (the original defect)
    killed    the clock fires ONCE -- returning false does not re-arm
    killed    the clock is gated on IsPlaying -- draining stops, so
              scrubbing breaks
    killed    the delta is discarded -- a zero tick advances nothing
    UNSCORED  the destructor leaves the clock registered      <-- re-run done;
                                                                  it CRASHES.
                                                                  see BELOW
    SURVIVED  IDENTITY CONTROL                                    (correct)

    killed 4  SURVIVED 1  INVALID 0  UNSCORED 1  skipped 0

The identity control SURVIVED, which is what makes the four kills readable at
all: the harness demonstrably let a no-op through, so the kills are about the
mutants rather than about a standing red. Baseline built clean and the suite
was green under this filter before any arm was applied -- mutate.sh prints both
and refuses to score otherwise.

ARM 1 IS THE ONE THAT MATTERED. "The clock is never registered" is the state
the codebase was in before #65, so a SURVIVED there would have meant the new
test could not see the very defect it was written for. It died.


WHICH ASSERTIONS KILLED WHICH ARM. ARM 1 IS READ. ARMS 2-4 ARE DERIVED.

The first campaign's evidence is gone. mutate.sh pointed BUILD_LOG and TEST_LOG
at one path each and every arm overwrote the last, so when it finished, only
arm 6's logs survived. session-workspace.README hit the same wall and asked for
a fix; this campaign ran a few hours too early to benefit from it. The fix
landed immediately afterwards as b7e2f7b (task #67, per-arm archived logs),
driven by Tools/tests/test_mutate_per_arm_logs.sh.

The re-run below then carried arm 1 as a control, WITH per-arm archiving in
place -- so arm 1's attribution is now quoted rather than reasoned. Arms 2-4
remain derived, and the difference is marked on each.

    clock never registered   ALL THREE ARMS -- READ, from the re-run's control
    (arm 1)  * READ *        A archive. Verbatim, five errors on one Fail:

                               Expected 'ticking only the ENGINE's core ticker
                               brings the display up on frame 0, so the
                               workspace registered a clock that reaches the
                               player (90 pump frames)' to be 0, but it was -1.

                               Expected 'and 60 further engine frames do NOT
                               move a PAUSED playhead...' to be 0, but it was -1.

                               Expected 'pressing Play and ticking only the
                               ENGINE advances the display past frame 0...;
                               reached frame -1 after 90 pump frames' to be true.

                               Expected 'and the physical time moved with it...'
                               to be true.

                               Expected 'a workspace built AFTER two others were
                               destroyed still gets its own clock...' to be 0,
                               but it was -1.

                             This confirms the derivation exactly: FrameA == -1
                             on all three test arms, because StartPendingLoads
                             is reachable only from Player::Tick, so with no
                             clock no load is ever started. It also matches the
                             pristine-run failure observed before #65 was
                             written, which is expected -- this arm restores the
                             pre-#65 state exactly.

What follows for arms 2-4 is reasoned from which assertions each mutant
necessarily breaks. That is weaker than quoting a log: it is a claim about what
a mutant SHOULD break, checked against nothing. Repo memory
a-compound-mutant-scores-like-a-narrow-one is exactly what it cannot see.

    returns false            DERIVED. Arms 1 and 3, on the SAME assertion as
    (arm 2)                  above but
                             for a different reason: the handler runs once,
                             which is not enough to complete a load and reach
                             a display. Arm 2 also fails (it needs many
                             ticks to advance past frame 0). Not narrowed
                             further, and it should have been -- see the gap
                             note below.

    gated on IsPlaying       DERIVED. Arms 1 and 3, and ONLY those two, which is
    (arm 3)                  why
                             the arm exists. A paused player never starts its
                             first load under this mutant, so "a freshly
                             opened workspace reaches frame 0" fails. Arm 2
                             CANNOT see it: it presses Play first, so the gate
                             is open and playback behaves correctly. An
                             implementation with this gate would look fully
                             working to anyone who only tested playback.

    delta discarded          DERIVED. Arm 2 ALONE, and this is the arm's whole
    (arm 4)                  point.
                             Advance() returns the playhead unchanged for a
                             non-positive delta, so draining and loading still
                             work and frame 0 still arrives -- arms 1 and 3
                             pass unchanged. Only "pressing Play advances the
                             display PAST frame 0" can fail. That is also why
                             arm 2 asserts FrameA > 0 rather than
                             FrameA != INDEX_NONE: frame 0 is the degenerate
                             value a paused player reaches, and the weaker
                             assertion would have let this mutant live.


THE UNSCORED ARM, AND WHY IT IS NOT BEING RECORDED AS A KILL.

    UNSCORED  the destructor leaves the clock registered
              FAIL: no tests matched 'FlowViz.UI.Workspace.ClockSeam'

"No tests matched" is what a dead editor looks like, not a result. classify_
test_run is right to refuse it: a crash and a failure are different facts and
want different actions (repo memory a-crash-reports-as-a-full-green).

The two candidate explanations were:

    (a) a machine blip -- an editor that died for unrelated reasons; or
    (b) the mutant crashing the editor, which is the defect the arm is about.

RE-RUN, batched with controls per repo memory rerun-unknowns-with-controls: an
UNSCORED re-run alone cannot separate (a) from (b), because a second blip looks
identical to a second crash. Tools/mutants/workspace-clock-rerun.txt runs it
alongside one already-killed arm and the identity control.

    killed    RE-RUN CONTROL A -- the clock is never registered
    UNSCORED  THE RE-RUN -- the destructor leaves the clock registered
                FAIL: no tests matched 'FlowViz.UI.Workspace.ClockSeam'
    SURVIVED  RE-RUN CONTROL B -- the identity control

Both controls behaved, so the harness was healthy and the second UNSCORED is
about this arm rather than about the machine. That rules out (a) as the general
explanation but does not by itself establish (b).

(b) IS ESTABLISHED, READ NOT DERIVED. The engine preserves a per-crash COPY of
the session log under

    ~/Library/Application Support/Epic/UnrealEngine/5.8/Saved/Crashes/
        CrashReport-UE-FlowViz-pid-<pid>-<guid>/FlowViz.log

keyed by PID, so unlike /tmp/flowviz_tests_<checkout>.log it is NOT overwritten
by the next arm. Two of them exist, one per campaign, and both say:

    LogMac: === Critical error: ===
    SIGSEGV: invalid attempt to access memory at address 0x0

    libUnrealEditor-FlowVizRuntime.dylib!FFlowVizCasePlayer::Tick(double)
    libUnrealEditor-Core.dylib!FTSTicker::Tick(float)
    UnrealEditor-Cmd!FEngineLoop::Tick()

A null dereference inside FFlowVizCasePlayer::Tick, reached FROM FTSTicker::Tick
-- which is the mutant's exact mechanism: the handle is never removed, the
handler keeps firing after the workspace is gone, and it ticks a freed model.

ATTRIBUTED BY TIME-BRACKETING, not by assumption:

    pid 29632   opened 11:08:02  crashed 11:08:23  exited 11:09:02
    arm 1 summary written 11:07:48  |  arm 2 summary written 11:09:04

The crashed session opens after arm 1 finished and exits two seconds before
arm 2's summary is written, so it is bracketed on both sides -- it is arm 2's.
The first campaign's pid 21782 is the same arm, same stack. Both ran with
-ExecCmds="Automation RunTests FlowViz.UI.Workspace.ClockSeam" and both logs
contain `Test Started. Path={FlowViz.UI.Workspace.ClockSeam}`, so the filter
MATCHED and the test STARTED. The crash landed between Started and Completed.

STILL NOT RECORDED AS A KILL, and the distinction is the whole point. The mutant
is DETECTED -- reliably, twice, by the same mechanism -- but it is not detected
by an ASSERTION. No TestTrue in this file goes red; the process dies. Recording
it as killed would credit the suite with a check it does not contain, and the
check it does not contain is the one that would survive somebody making the
crash not-crash (a null guard in Tick would silence this while leaving the
dangling registration in place).

The test file already says so, and says it better than a verdict could:

    It doubles as the crash probe for the opposite error -- a clock that keeps
    firing into a destroyed FFlowVizWorkspaceModel. That failure is a
    use-after-free, which no TestTrue can observe: it either reads plausible
    garbage or takes the session down [...] So the probe is the pump; the
    assertions below are about the live instance and can fail on their own.

So the arm is working as designed and UNSCORED is the honest verdict. What the
campaign has actually established is that the pump reaches the dangling handler
-- which is worth more than a verdict, because it means the probe is live rather
than vacuous.

WHAT THIS EXPOSED IN THE TOOLING, now filed as its own tasks:

    #68  run_tests.sh prints "FAIL: no tests matched '<filter>'. Check the test
         path." for a crashed session. The crash detection added by #61 sits at
         line 194, INSIDE the truncation block, which is unreachable after the
         RAN==0 early exit at 127. A crash, a crash-before-any-test, and a
         genuine typo all produce byte-identical output. The message sent me to
         check a filter that was always correct. #61 fixed one branch of this
         hazard and left the other -- repo memory
         audit-the-second-branch-of-every-documented-hazard, on our own tools.

    #69  run_tests.sh's engine log path is per-CHECKOUT, not per-run, so all
         three arms' archived summaries end with the SAME "Full log:" pointer
         and only the last arm's session survives in it. #67 archived the
         summaries; it did not archive what they point at.

#69 nearly cost a false claim in this very document: I opened that shared log
looking for arm 2, found Result={Success} and EXIT CODE: 0, and briefly
concluded arm 2 had run and passed. Its mtime (11:09:44) is arm 3's -- the
identity control. Repo memory shared-tree-results-need-a-timestamp caught it,
and the crash reports above are what settled the question instead, precisely
because they are keyed by pid rather than by checkout.


THE GAP THIS CAMPAIGN LEAVES, stated because it is the kind that reads as
covered.

Arm 2 ("returns false") is a COMPOUND arm by accident: it breaks all three test
arms at once, so whichever is checked first accounts for it and the other two
ride along. A narrower mutant -- one that re-arms for a bounded number of ticks
rather than never -- would have separated "the clock runs at all" from "the
clock keeps running", and nothing here demonstrates the second. Repo memory
a-compound-mutant-scores-like-a-narrow-one.

And the campaign as a whole stops at the DISPLAY SELECTION, because that is
where the test stops. No arm here could show that a playing case reaches the
GPU, and in fact it does not: FFlowVizCasePlayer::SetTextureSet has no caller
anywhere in the module, so the player's texture set is null and its upload is
skipped. The clock, the playhead and the dirty-marking are all correct and all
point at texture slots nothing wrote. That is task #66, and it is invisible to
every assertion in this file.
